WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.257
A schema is also a contract for future users.

00:00:03.257 --> 00:00:11.362
Consistent naming, named constraints, ERDs, and a data dictionary make the structure easier to understand.

00:00:11.362 --> 00:00:14.997
Change management matters because schemas evolve.

00:00:14.997 --> 00:00:20.857
Use versioned migrations, review important changes, and test how to roll them back.

00:00:20.857 --> 00:00:25.667
Security includes least privilege, separation of sensitive data, and access auditing.

00:00:25.667 --> 00:00:32.527
Reliability includes backups, restore testing, quality monitoring, ownership, and lineage.

00:00:32.527 --> 00:00:34.827
For analytics, document more than column names.

00:00:34.827 --> 00:00:41.712
Record table grain, source, owner, refresh behavior, and how the data moves from source systems into

00:00:41.712 --> 00:00:43.142
analytical outputs.

00:00:43.142 --> 00:00:51.357
Good governance helps people trust not only the database structure, but also the meaning and history of the

00:00:51.357 --> 00:00:58.222
data. Connect this idea to a university system separating students, courses, sections, and enrollments.

00:00:58.222 --> 00:01:03.422
Ask what could go wrong if the concept were misunderstood: could rows be duplicated,

00:01:03.422 --> 00:01:11.822
important records disappear, categories be interpreted incorrectly, or a report become difficult to reproduce?

00:01:11.822 --> 00:01:16.582
A strong analyst can explain both the SQL operation and the assumption behind it.

00:01:16.582 --> 00:01:22.880
After the example, summarize the idea in one plain-language sentence before moving on.
